    {
      "by": "FarhanAliRaza",
      "at": "2026-10-16T19:57:04Z",
      "excerpt": "Valid — fixed in dae5bf47e9 (same finding as the cubic thread). aenter now sets self mutable , self entered context and wrapped before the rehydrate, so a failure or a cancellation goes through aexit and closes the state manager context. Regression test: test "
    },
    {
      "by": "FarhanAliRaza",
      "at": "2026-10-16T19:57:07Z",
      "excerpt": "Valid, but it is older than this PR, so it is out of scope here. The foreground rehydrate on main has the same limit: State.hydrate serializes only the attached substates, and with Redis an event loads only the ancestors and descendants of its handler state. T"
    },
    {
      "by": "FarhanAliRaza",
      "at": "2026-10-16T19:57:08Z",
      "excerpt": "Valid — fixed in 1792b36f9e. The test now sets oplock enabled = False on the Redis manager, as the dispatch-tree test does. With oplock the tree stays cached in the process, so deleting the keys did not expire it. Checked with REFLEX OPLOCK ENABLED=true : the "
    },
    {
      "by": "FarhanAliRaza",
      "at": "2026-10-19T09:32:28Z",
      "excerpt": "Valid — fixed in 95148bc18c. The background re-entry no longer copies the task's start route into the expired tree. The rehydrate stays routeless, so no stale router values reach the browser and no on load runs for the old page. The client's next event carries"
    }
